{"id": "56efd71e-8f5a-4ca8-a8fe-cf33e2058947", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:35:29.237587", "source": "agent", "parent_id": null, "system_prompt": {"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Collaborate with the researcher to develop an experiment design for the original task. First inspect the masked repository and supplied inputs, then use end_researcher_turn with stage=plan to propose an approach, consequential choices and uncertainties. Wait for explicit researcher approval before developing the full design. After approval, hand off stage=result with the complete design in answer, supported by rationale, controls, feasible checks and repository observations in evidence. State assumptions separately from fixed task requirements. No executed experiment or invented outcome is required for this design-only task. Use inspect_repository and execute_check for exploration. Share concrete findings: private tool output is not automatically sent to the researcher. Revise when needed; do not invent objections or ambiguities to extend the conversation. The researcher decides when to stop."}, "tools": [{"description": "List a repository directory or read a text file. Paths are relative to the task workspace.", "action_type": "InspectRepositoryAction", "observation_type": "BenchmarkObservation", "annotations": null, "meta": null, "kind": "InspectRepositoryTool", "title": "inspect_repository"}, {"description": "Run a shell command in the isolated task workspace. Use small exploratory checks before submitting your plan.", "action_type": "ExecuteCheckAction", "observation_type": "BenchmarkObservation", "annotations": null, "meta": null, "kind": "ExecuteCheckTool", "title": "execute_check"}, {"description": "Yield a proposed approach (stage=plan) or completed experiment design (stage=result), with evidence, assumptions and uncertainties. The researcher decides when to finish.", "action_type": "ReviewHandoffAction", "observation_type": "BenchmarkObservation", "annotations": null, "meta": null, "kind": "ReviewHandoffTool", "title": "end_researcher_turn"}], "dynamic_context": {"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "<CURRENT_DATETIME>\nThe current date and time is: 2026-10-22T16:34\n</CURRENT_DATETIME>"}, "kind": "SystemPromptEvent"}
{"id": "f3a44094-67ad-4770-90a0-76d46d18d802", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:35:29.239203", "source": "user", "parent_id": "56efd71e-8f5a-4ca8-a8fe-cf33e2058947", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Before any design work, please perform a scope-and-inspection pass only — do not produce the experiment design yet.\\n\\n1) Inputs and environment inspection: confirm what is actually available in /workspace (any code, datasets, prior attack/suffix artifacts, harnesses, eval scripts), the runtime constraints stated in the project context (bubblewrap sandbox, network disabled, no GPU, 224 CPU affinity), and note explicitly that no model weights, API access, or reference suffix corpora are preinstalled. Report what you found versus what is absent, rather than assuming assets exist.\\n\\n2) Proposed approach at a high level only: sketch the shape of the design you would propose for the causal question \\\"how does adversarial suffix length affect ASR, and is there a saturation point?\\\". Cover at minimum: (a) how suffix length will be operationalized and manipulated independently of other properties (e.g. optimization budget, token count vs character count, whether longer suffixes are produced by longer optimization runs), (b) candidate attack families in scope (e.g. GCG-style optimized suffixes, transferable/prefixed suffixes, random or padding-controlled suffixes), (c) which dependent variable and ASR scoring you would use, (d) how you would model and test for saturation (e.g. plateau detection, asymptotic/asigmoid or piecewise model, change-point or first-differences criterion), and (e) what would count as a control condition. Keep this to the approach outline — I want to review the design choices before you detail the full protocol, controls matrix, analysis plan, and feasibility budget.\\n\\n3) Assumptions and uncertainties: list consequential assumptions you would be making, especially any that are not supported by the project brief (the brief leaves model family/size, number of prompts, attack implementation, compute budget, and ASR judge unspecified). For each, mark it clearly as an assumption or an open question rather than a established fact from the brief. Also flag known confounds in this literature area that your design must handle — in particular the coupling between suffix length and optimization steps/loss, prompt-specific vs universal suffixes, and refusal-judge false positives.\\n\\nDo not claim any experiment was run, and do not report results or effect sizes; this task is design-only. Keep the reply structured so I can review the approach and then either approve it or ask for one focused revision.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How does the **length of adversarial suffix** used in token-level jailbreak attacks affect the **Attack Success Rate (ASR)** of LLMs? Specifically, is there a saturation point beyond which longer suffixes no longer improve attack effectiveness?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 224, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
