{"id": "11a49b34-b396-4f50-8d77-ca81cc210e70", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:37:23.252754", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "9ce3e385-aacf-4038-b405-05f4bac1ea75", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Environment confirmed. Next, I'll create a plan-stage handoff containing the requested structure. Design only, no results. Concise yet comprehensive.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "SCOPE-AND-INSPECTION PASS + HIGH-LEVEL APPROACH SKETCH (design only; nothing executed as an experiment).\n\n=== 1) ENVIRONMENT / INPUTS: FOUND vs ABSENT (verified by direct inspection) ===\nFOUND:\n- /workspace contains exactly one file: task.json (the masked task spec; includes note \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\").\n- bubblewrap 0.11.1 is installed (runtime constraint in brief is consistent with reality).\n- python3 = Python 3.14.4; 224 visible CPUs; ~2 TB RAM; /tmp is a 1 TB tmpfs.\n- A general-purpose system Python with 105 packages (ansible, Jinja2, cryptography, etc.) — an unrelated distro-style environment.\nABSENT (explicitly verified, not assumed):\n- No code repository, no attack implementation, no harness, no eval/scoring scripts, no datasets (brief's \"datasets\": [] confirmed).\n- No prior suffix/attack artifacts anywhere searched (/workspace, /home, /srv, /opt, /models, /data — none of the latter three exist).\n- No torch/transformers or any ML dependency; no GPU (/dev/nvidia* absent); no huggingface cache.\n- Network disabled (DNS resolution failure confirmed; pip download fails). So no model weights, no API access, no reference suffix corpora (e.g., no AdvBench/HarmBench copy), no pip-installable dependencies in this sandbox.\nCONSEQUENCE: this is a pure design deliverable. Any executable protocol must be specified against external compute the brief does not provide; feasibility numbers are parameterized, not claimed.\n\n=== 2) APPROACH SKETCH (for review before detailing) ===\n(a) Operationalizing suffix length independently:\n- Primary unit: TOKEN count of the suffix under the target model's tokenizer (chars reported as secondary). Length grid L ∈ {8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512} tokens (grid adjustable), treated as a designed factor, not a byproduct.\n- Decoupling length from optimization budget is the core design commitment. Two complementary manipulations:\n  * Length-constrained optimization (\"fixed-L\"): for each L, run the SAME optimization procedure — identical initialization scheme (last-L tokens of a fixed boilerplate string), identical per-step coordinate-flip budget (e.g., K candidate flips per step), identical max optimization STEPS T, identical loss (NLL of affirmative target string) — differing only in the width of the suffix slot. Optimize suffixes per model family so tokenizer matches.\n  * Step-budget control (\"isototal\"): a secondary arm matching TOTAL token-flip updates (T scaled so total candidate evaluations ≈ constant across L), so we can separate \"longer slot\" effects from \"more effective optimization at longer L\". If ASR rises with L even at fixed T and fixed total-update-budget, that is a length effect, not an optimization-effort artifact.\n- All suffixes appended to prompt end with the same join formatting; forbidden-token filtering identical across L.\n\n(b) Attack families in scope (2 core + 2 controls):\n  1. GCG-style per-prompt optimized suffixes (white-box, greedy coordinate flips) — primary.\n  2. Universal/transfer suffixes: optimize one suffix per family at a reference L on surrogate model(s), transfer to targets; length variants produced by re-optimizing at each L under the fixed recipe (transferability vs length is itself a saturation-relevant curve).\n  3. Control family: RANDOM token suffixes matched in length (uniform over vocab, filtered to decodable strings) — length-matched non-adversarial baseline.\n  4. Control family: SEMANTIC-NULL optimized suffixes — run the identical optimizer against a non-harmful \"benign completion\" target (harmless prompt + its true continuation), producing same-length suffixes with no jailbreak intent — controls for \"long gibberish itself perturbs generation\" effects.\n\n(c) Dependent variable and ASR scoring:\n- Primary DV: per-condition ASR = fraction of (prompt × seed) trials judged successful.\n- Primary judge: string-match refusal classifier (refusal-prefix/keyword list from the standard jailbreak literature, applied to full generation) — cheap, deterministic, reproducible.\n- Secondary judge: an LLM-as-judge safety classifier on a stratified random subsample of generations, with a manually double-labeled ~200-generation calibration set to estimate judge false-positive rate (compliance vs mere topic mention), reported alongside primary ASR.\n- Generation protocol fixed: greedy or one shared sampling temperature, fixed max_new_tokens (long enough to expose refusal mid-stream), same system prompt / chat template across conditions.\n- Units of analysis: trial-level binary outcome; ASR aggregated over prompt set (advversarial behavior prompts sourced from a standard harm prompt set — specified as an acquisition step since no corpus exists locally).\n\n(d) Saturation modeling and testing (pre-registered decision rules):\n- Descriptive: mean ASR per L with binomial CIs; first-differences between adjacent L levels with multiplicity control — saturation = point where adjacent-L differences lose significance and CI bands overlap.\n- Model-based: fit a saturating curve ASR(L) = a + (b−a)·(1−exp(−L/λ)) (and a log-logistic variant) via nonlinear mixed-effects or per-model fits; saturation point estimated as L* = smallest L whose fitted ASR is within Δ (e.g., 2 percentage points) of the asymptote b. Compare saturating model vs linear model by BIC/cross-validated log-loss: saturation claim requires the saturating family to win.\n- Piecewise/changepoint (segmented regression) as a triangulation estimate of the breakpoint; report L* with bootstrap CI across prompts.\n- Report curves separately per model and per attack family; saturation is claimed only if consistent across ≥2 model families and the fixed-T arm.\n\n(e) Control conditions (summary): random-length-matched suffixes; benign-target optimized suffixes; empty-suffix (prompt-only) baseline to bound baseline ASR; fixed-steps vs scaled-steps arms for the optimization-effort confound; a refusal-style system prompt / \"prefix defense\" spot-check to confirm measured ASR reflects the attack, not template artifacts; judge calibration set for false positives.\n\nTargets: 2–3 open-weight chat-tuned models spanning two architectures and ≥2 sizes (model choice is an open question — see below), plus one held-out model for transfer testing. Nothing is preinstalled; the design must state model acquisition as a prerequisite on external compute.\n\n=== 3) ASSUMPTIONS AND OPEN QUESTIONS ===\nAssumptions (A) vs open questions (Q):\n- A1: External compute exists for the eventual run; THIS sandbox (no GPU, no network, no ML libs) can host code and analysis only. The brief's 224-CPU/2TB-RAM box is adequate for the analysis stage and possibly tiny-model (≤1B CPU) pilot of the pipeline, but that is a capability claim about piloting plumbing, not the experiment.\n- A2: Open-weight white-box models are usable (needed for GCG gradients). Brief does not specify model family/size — OPEN: which models (candidate shape: two 7–13B chat models from two families + one small held-out).\n- A3: A standard public harm-prompt set (AdvBench-style, ~50–120 behaviors) will be acquired externally — OPEN: none is local; count is unspecified in the brief. My planning default: 100 prompts × ~5 seeds.\n- A4: Attack implementation will be a faithful re-implementation of GCG greedy coordinate ascent (reference code must be ported; none local) — assumption that re-implementation, not the authors' exact code, will be used.\n- A5: Brief specifies NO ASR judge, NO compute budget, NO prompt count, NO model family — all four are design choices I am making, not facts from the brief. Flagging explicitly.\n- A6: Fixed per-step flip budget K and step count T independent of L is accepted as the \"equal effort\" definition; reasonable but contestable.\nKnown confounds the design must handle (from the literature):\n- C1: Length↔optimization coupling — longer suffixes give the optimizer more coordinates/loss reduction; handled by fixed-T, isototal-update, and step-budget arms.\n- C2: Per-prompt vs universal suffixes behave differently with length (universal suffixes historically optimized at L≈20); handled by separate curves per family, never pooled.\n- C3: Refusal-judge false positives/negatives — keyword matchers miss late refusals and flag compliant-then-refusing or hedged answers; handled by two-tier judging + calibration subset + full-generation scoring + fixed max_new_tokens.\n- C4: Tokenizer differences across models make \"same L\" not \"same string\"; handled by optimizing per model in its own tokenizer and reporting char length and perplexity of suffix as covariates.\n- C5: Gibberish-vs-semantics: longer optimized suffixes drift toward fluent text; the benign-target and random controls isolate intent-specific effects.\n- C6: Sampling variance — greedy vs sampled generation changes apparent ASR; one fixed decoding protocol, seeds as random effects.", "evidence": "Inspection commands run in this sandbox (all exploratory, no experiment):\n- `ls -la /workspace; find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f` → only task.json (539 B). task.json contains the question plus documents.track.md: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n- `python3 -c \"import torch, transformers\"` → ModuleNotFoundError (no ML stack). `pip list | wc -l` → 105 packages, none ML-related.\n- `nproc` → 224; `free -g` → ~2 TB RAM; `df -h` → /tmp 1 TB tmpfs; `ls /dev/nvidia*` → absent (no GPU).\n- Network probe: urllib request to pypi.org → URLError \"Temporary fai