AdvBench-style, ~50\\u2013120 behaviors) will be acquired externally \\u2014 OPEN: none is local; count is unspecified in the brief. My planning default: 100 prompts \\u00d7 ~5 seeds.\\n- A4: Attack implementation will be a faithful re-implementation of GCG greedy coordinate ascent (reference code must be ported; none local) \\u2014 assumption that re-implementation, not the authors' exact code, will be used.\\n- A5: Brief specifies NO ASR judge, NO compute budget, NO prompt count, NO model family \\u2014 all four are design choices I am making, not facts from the brief. Flagging explicitly.\\n- A6: Fixed per-step flip budget K and step count T independent of L is accepted as the \\\"equal effort\\\" definition; reasonable but contestable.\\nKnown confounds the design must handle (from the literature):\\n- C1: Length\\u2194optimization coupling \\u2014 longer suffixes give the optimizer more coordinates/loss reduction; handled by fixed-T, isototal-update, and step-budget arms.\\n- C2: Per-prompt vs universal suffixes behave differently with length (universal suffixes historically optimized at L\\u224820); handled by separate curves per family, never pooled.\\n- C3: Refusal-judge false positives/negatives \\u2014 keyword matchers miss late refusals and flag compliant-then-refusing or hedged answers; handled by two-tier judging + calibration subset + full-generation scoring + fixed max_new_tokens.\\n- C4: Tokenizer differences across models make \\\"same L\\\" not \\\"same string\\\"; handled by optimizing per model in its own tokenizer and reporting char length and perplexity of suffix as covariates.\\n- C5: Gibberish-vs-semantics: longer optimized suffixes drift toward fluent text; the benign-target and random controls isolate intent-specific effects.\\n- C6: Sampling variance \\u2014 greedy vs sampled generation changes apparent ASR; one fixed decoding protocol, seeds as random effects.\", \"evidence\": \"Inspection commands run in this sandbox (all exploratory, no experiment):\\n- `ls -la /workspace; find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f` \\u2192 only task.json (539 B). task.json contains the question plus documents.track.md: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n- `python3 -c \\\"import torch, transformers\\\"` \\u2192 ModuleNotFoundError (no ML stack). `pip list | wc -l` \\u2192 105 packages, none ML-related.\\n- `nproc` \\u2192 224; `free -g` \\u2192 ~2 TB RAM; `df -h` \\u2192 /tmp 1 TB tmpfs; `ls /dev/nvidia*` \\u2192 absent (no GPU).\\n- Network probe: urllib request to pypi.org \\u2192 URLError \\\"Temporary failure in name resolution\\\"; `pip download numpy` \\u2192 fails. Network confirmed disabled; no way to fetch weights, datasets, or reference suffix corpora here.\\n- `ls /opt /models /data ~/.cache/huggingface` \\u2192 none exist; targeted find for *suffix*/*gcg*/*jailbreak* artifacts under /workspace, /home, /srv \\u2192 empty.\\n- `bwrap --version` \\u2192 bubblewrap 0.11.1 present, consistent with the stated runtime.\", \"assumptions\": [\"External GPU compute exists for eventual execution; this sandbox is design/analysis-only (not stated in brief)\", \"Open-weight white-box chat models will be selected by the researcher; brief does not specify model family or size\", \"A standard public harm-prompt corpus (~100 behaviors, AdvBench-style) will be acquired externally; brief specifies no datasets or prompt count\", \"GCG will be re-implemented faithfully rather than run via original author code (none local, network disabled)\", \"Brief leaves ASR judge, compute budget, prompt count, and model selection unspecified; my defaults (string-match primary judge + LLM-judge calibration, 100 prompts x ~5 seeds, two model families) are design choices, not facts\", \"Equal-effort across lengths is defined as identical optimizer steps T and per-step flip budget K, with an isototal-updates arm as robustness\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Whether fixed-T truly equalizes attack difficulty across suffix lengths, or whether an isototal or wall-clock budget is the better equal-effort definition (design hedges by running both arms)\", \"Which saturation criterion (first-differences, asymptotic fit, changepoint) the researcher prefers as primary; plan pre-registers all three with the saturating-vs-linear model comparison as decisive\", \"Whether universal-suffix length curves should be in the primary analysis or exploratory secondary (they scale compute roughly 2x)\", \"Feasibility budget cannot be validated in-sandbox; per-cell GCG cost estimates depend on the unresolved model-size choice\", \"Judge calibration: whether an open judge model is available on the execution cluster for the secondary LLM-as-judge tier\"]}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
{"id": "5de496da-e0d2-42d0-a29c-d7b23c04fac0", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:37:23.258706", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "11a49b34-b396-4f50-8d77-ca81cc210e70", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8d55fb2b7e581a38", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Handoff recorded; waiting for researcher review."}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "11a49b34-b396-4f50-8d77-ca81cc210e70", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "14132415-e26a-4351-b901-1bc47610c2a6", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:38:39.798683", "source": "user", "parent_id": "5de496da-e0d2-42d0-a29c-d7b23c04fac0", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved. The inspection is credible (empty /workspace apart from task.json, no ML stack, no GPU, no network), and your framing correctly treats the length~ASR question as a confounded causal question rather than a curve-plotting exercise. The fixed-T vs isototal decoupling, separate curves per attack family, length-matched random and benign-target optimized controls, two-tier judging with a calibration subset, and tokenizer-matched per-model optimization are all the right structural commitments. Proceed to the full design deliverable. Carry these five decisions/clarifications into it explicitly — they resolve the open choices you flagged rather than leaving them to be discovered later:\\n\\n1) Equal-effort definition: make fixed-T (identical steps T and per-step flip budget K across L) the PRIMARY arm and isototal the robustness arm, and state why (fixed-T matches the standard reporting convention in the literature, so the primary curve is comparable to published numbers; isototal answers the \\\"was it just effort\\\" objection). Report both curves side by side, never pooled. Pre-commit to the interpretation rule: a length effect is credited only if the ASR-vs-L ordering holds in both arms.\\n\\n2) Length grid: extend downward as well as upward. Add short levels (e.g. 1, 2, 4 tokens) alongside {8...512}, and include at least one level clearly past the expected plateau. Rationale: a saturation claim needs the low-L regime where the curve is still rising (otherwise you cannot distinguish \\\"saturated\\\" from \\\"already at ceiling everywhere\\\") and the non-monotone possibility (very long suffixes can degrade ASR as they dominate the context or become more detectable/refusal-prone). Specify how you will report a non-monotone or decreasing tail rather than forcing it into a monotone asymptote.\\n\\n3) Saturation criterion: designate ONE primary criterion and label the rest confirmatory. I recommend the model-based criterion as primary (saturating family must beat linear/log-linear by BIC or CV log-loss, with L* defined by the within-Delta-of-asymptote rule), with first-differences and segmented-regression changepoint as triangulation, because the first-differences criterion is sensitive to the prompt-count/sample size and will declare \\\"saturation\\\" under low power. Note that Delta must be justified as a practically meaningful ASR difference, not chosen post hoc; state your planned Delta and the power/sensitivity check for the prompt count you assume.\\n\\n4) Universal-suffix arm: keep it as a pre-registered SECONDARY analysis, not primary, given roughly doubled compute and the fact that the primary question is about optimized suffix length. Justify this and state what a surprising universal result would and would not license you to conclude.\\n\\n5) Feasibility: parameterize the cost model rather than asserting numbers — give per-cell GCG cost as a function of model size, T, K, prompt count and seeds, plus a scaling table for 2-3 concrete model-size scenarios, and identify the dominant cost driver and what you would cut first if the budget is constrained (and what that cut would compromise). Be explicit that all of this is a specification for external compute, not something run here.\\n\\nAlso deliver the controls as an explicit matrix (condition x what it rules out x expected pattern if the length effect is genuine x interpretation if it fails), and include a short limitations section covering what this design cannot settle (e.g. whether saturation is a property of the attack optimizer versus the model's refusal mechanism, and the generalization limits of an open-weight white-box sample). Do not report or imply any results, effect sizes, or executed runs — this remains design-only.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "edb0e217-6987-4dcb-9b80-300323603e51", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:41:06.351041", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "14132415-e26a-4351-b901-1bc47610c2a6", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The researcher has approved and wants the full design deliverable at stage=result. I should create a comprehensive protocol incorporating the 5 decisions, control matrix, feasibility cost model with scaling table, limitations, and analysis plan. No results. Should I do additional investigation? Not really necessary. But I could do a quick sanity computation for the cost model (e.g., Python arithmetic for the scaling table)—that's arithmetic, not experimentation. Useful for getting the numbers in the table to line up.\n\nLet me build the cost model:\n\nCost of GCG per prompt / per suffix:\n- Forward passes per step: K candidate token flips each require a forward pass over the prompt+suffix+target sequence. Standard GCG uses a gradient trick to rank candidate substitutions (compute gradient of loss w.r.t. one-hot suffix embeddings and multiply with embedding matrix to get ~|V| loss deltas), then evaluates top-P (e.g., 32 or K) candidates with full forward passes; picks best. So per-step cost ≈ (one forward+backward) + P forward passes, P = number of candidates evaluated. Define K = candidates evaluated per step (P in my notation). Steps T.\n- Sequence length N ≈ prompt (~100 tokens) + suffix L + target (~30 tokens) + template overhead. Attention cost grows with N, but linear term dominates for moderate N.\n\nCost per cell (model M with per-forward-pass cost c(N)): trials × T × (K+2) forward-equivalents. Forward-pass FLOPs ≈ 2·P_params·N for decode-style forward. So time per forward on GPU ≈ 2·P·N / (effective throughput). Effective throughput ≈ 50% of peak; for a one-off forward, assume it's memory-bound for small batches: time ≈ model bytes / memory bandwidth + compute. Simplification: present a parameterized formula and give illustrative per-model numbers in GPU-hours per 100 prompts × 5 seeds with T, K specified... wait: seeds in GCG—seeds affect initialization. GCG is near-deterministic; seeds vary initialization. But generation-decoding seeds are also relevant. Trials = prompts × seeds.\n\nLet me parameterize:\n\nCost_cell (FLOPs) ≈ C_trials × T × (K + 2) × 2·M_params·N_avg × (forward+backward factor ~1.5 on the gradient step)\nwhere C_trials = prompts × seeds; N_avg = prompt + L + target + template.\n\nActually, per step there's 1 fwd+bwd (≈3× forward FLOPs) + K forwards for candidate evaluation. So FLOPs_per_step ≈ (3 + K)·2·M·N.\n\nIllustrative table: M ∈ {2B, 7B, 13B} scenarios (chat-tuned), effective throughput assumption: sustained 35% of peak; A100 bf16 312 TFLOPs → effective ≈ 110 TFLOPs. T=500, K=64 (candidate evaluations per step; typical top-k per position is 256 but number evaluated is 32–128; pick K=64), prompts=100, seeds... GCG optimization seeds: 1 optimization per (prompt, L) plus generation seeds for DV? ASR trials are decoding: greedy is deterministic; if we treat decoding as fixed greedy, then \"seeds\" are optimization init seeds. Set s=3 optimization seeds. C = 300.\n\nCompute per model, per L level:\nFLOPs = 300 × T(500) × (64+3) × 2·M·N.\nN at L=64: prompt 100 + suffix 64 + target 30 + template 40 ≈ 234. N varies with L; use N ≈ 170 + L.\n\nCompute for 7B, N=234: 2·7e9·234 = 3.276e12 FLOPs / forward. ×67 = 2.195e14 / step. ×500 = 1.097e17 per trial. ×300 = 3.29e19 FLOPs per L level. At 110e12 FLOPs/s → 3.0e5 s ≈ 83 GPU-hours per L level. ×10 L levels = 830 GPU-hours. Hmm, this is on the high side but plausible; literature GCG is ~1–2 GPU-hours per prompt at 500 steps on 7B? 83 GPU-h / 300 trials = 0.28 GPU-h/trial. Actual reported GCG runs are on the order of minutes per prompt on A100; 0.28h=17min, reasonable given K=64 evals.\n\nLet me compute all three sizes with a simple script for the table, to keep the numbers consistent. Also 2B, 13B. Also identify the dominant driver: K×T×M. First to cut: reduce L levels? No—the first cut is K (candidate evals), then seeds, then prompts, then T; explain what each cut compromises.\n\nAlso power/sensitivity for Δ: prompt count n=100, ASR difference near plateau. For first-differences: adjacent level paired (same prompts)—McNemar. Detectable paired difference at n=100, α=.05, power .8: need discordance rate ~? Under McNemar with 100 paired binary, detectable |ΔASR| ≈ 0.15–0.20 depending on discordance. This is why first-differences alone underpowered; model-based criterion pools all levels. Set Δ = 3 percentage points as practical significance (state justification: below typical inter-seed/decoding variability and below differences practitioners care about). But detecting 3pp needs large n: for independent binomial at n=100, comparing to asymptote... honestly state that n=100 prompts can't resolve 3pp differences at adjacent levels; that's precisely why the primary test is the model comparison (saturating vs linear)—i.e., curvature detectable from coarse differences across the whole curve—and L* CIs come from bootstrap. Also propose optional prompt count escalation and simulation-based power analysis before running the experiment (pre-registration requirement).\n\nCompute scenario table: also give formula and numbers at fixed T=500, K=64. And a scaling table across {2B, 7B, 13B}. Note also per-level N scaling; cost varies ~linearly with N; sum over 10 levels of {1,2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256,512}.\n\nCompute per model = Σ_L C·T·(K+3)·2·M·(170+L) (approx) ≈ C·T·(K+3)·2·M·(170·10 + ΣL). ΣL = 1019. So mean N ≈ 272 per level... fine, script it.\n\nAlso cost for evaluation: post-optimization ASR eval—1 generation per (prompt, L, seed) trial, ~200–512 new tokens; relatively cheap compared to optimization. Include it.\n\nCost for universal arm: per family optimize on surrogate at each L (1 universal suffix per L per family—cheap optimization: T steps but one pass over batch of 32 prompts; per-step cost ×32), plus transfer eval: targets × prompts trials. Secondary; ~1.5–2× eval cost of primary but low optimization cost. State ~2× total compute as previously claimed; refine.\n\nAlso non-monotonic reporting rule (decision 2): if quadratic term / changepoint suggests descent, allow non-monotone model family (e.g., log-logistic with overshoot, quadratic in log L, or monotone-constrained vs unconstrained comparison); report tail slope over last two levels with CI; don't force L* if asymptote b is below observed max ASR, instead report \"peak at L_peak, degradation beyond.\"\n\nJustification of Δ: set Δ = 3 pp.\n\nPower check: pre-registration step—simulate paired logistic model under plausible ranges of prompt-level heterogeneity to check power for (i) saturating vs linear discrimination at n=100 across 10 lengths with 3 seeds, and (ii) coverage of L* bootstrap CI. Escalation rule: if power <0.8 for Δ=3pp under pessimistic variance, increase prompts to 200 or reduce levels to 7 to fund. Hmm—budget: primary arm only.\n\nControl matrix: rows: empty suffix; random tokens of matched length; benign-target optimization of matched length; fixed-T vs isototal arm; prefix/system-prompt defense spot-check; refusal-substring judge vs LLM judge calibration; plus held-out model transfer. Columns: condition, what it rules out, expected pattern if length effect genuine, interpretation if fails.\n\nModels: primary white-box 2 families × sizes: candidate shapes: Llama-3-8B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (or Gemma-2-9B-it), held-out transfer target Mistral-7B-Instruct; optional small 1B (Llama-3.2-1B/3B-Instruct) for pipeline validation on this CPU box. Also one larger (e.g., Llama-3.1-70B? probably too costly—list as stretch). State model choice as a decision finalized here per researcher's instruction? Researcher said model choice was open but asked to parameterize for 2–3 size scenarios. Propose primary configuration: two 7–9B models from two families + one held-out 7B from a third family; sizes {2B,7B,13B} for cost scenarios.\n\nDeliverables/protocol steps: environment setup on external compute, porting/validating GCG implementation (validation checks: reproduce known GCG ASR trend at L=20 on AdvBench subset on at least one model—careful: this is a validation milestone, not a claimed result; frame as pre-flight validation with pass criterion \"optimizer reduces loss and produces suffixes that at L=20 exceed empty-suffix ASR by >0\"—fine as design). Forbidden tokens list (refusal words, \"sorry\" etc.—standard). Chat template handling.\n\nAnalysis plan: logistic mixed model per family: success ~ f(L) + (1|prompt) + (1|seed...); model families: exponential saturation, log-logistic, piecewise linear, linear, log-linear, quadratic in log L. Compare with BIC and LOOCV (leave-one-prompt-out CV log loss). Primary test = model-family comparison + within-Δ rule to extract L* with bootstrap CI (resample prompts, 2000 reps). Pre-registered decision rule: (i) saturating beats linear/log-linear by ΔBIC≥10 and CV log loss; (ii) same ordering in fixed-T and isototal arms; (iii) random and benign controls flat across L → conclude saturation exists and estimate L*. Otherwise alternatives.\n\nNon-monotone rule: fit unconstrained quadratic in log2(L); if right tail slope (last three levels) negative with 95% CI excluding 0, report peak L and tail decline; L* reported as rising-phase plateau onset with explicit \"no asymptote—peak\" flag; use hinged model.\n\nASR scoring details: refusal substring list (applied to first 512 tokens and full text); two-stage: substring primary across all trials; LLM judge on stratified subsample (stratify by cell; ~20 cells × 40 samples = 800 samples; manual double-label 200 samples, report Cohen's kappa, adjust primary via correction matrix (bias correction estimates)). Exclude outputs that are unfinished from ASR denominator? No—fixed max_new_tokens=512, scored on full generation.\n\nAlso include data schema and outputs: trial-level CSV schema; pre-registration document; artifact release (suffix corpus itself becomes artifact).\n\nRandomness/seeds; forbidden-token constraint identical; per-model tokenizer; record character length, suffix perplexity under model as covariates.\n\nFeasibility: run Python arithmetic now for internal table consistency. Let me do a quick computation.\n\nAssumptions: effective throughput E = 0.35 × A100 312 TFLOPs ≈ 109 TFLOPs for small-batch forward-dominated workload (probably conservative). Per-trial fwd+bwd factor: 1 fwd+bwd (3×) + K fwd per step. K=64.\n\nCost per trial·step FLOPs = (3 + K) × 2·M·N.\n\nC=300 trials per cell (100 prompts × 3 optimization seeds). Wait—do I need 3 optimization seeds for 100×3? GCG is stochastic via initialization + sampling of candidate ranking; 3 seeds reasonable. Generation: greedy decoding, deterministic given suffix → ASR trial count = 300 per (model, L, family/arm).\n\nTotal optimization GPU-hours per model = Σ_L C·T·(K+3)·2·M·(170+L)/E... note K+3: (K evals + fwd+bwd counted as 3). Use factor (K+3).\n\nEval cost: generations 300/level ×10 levels; cost ≈ 2·M·(N + 512) per token, average ~... ≈ 2·M·(234+256 avg position)... simple: per generation ≈ 2·M·(N_prompt_suffix·1 + n_gen) ≈ 2·M·(234 + 512) ≈ negligible: 300×10×2×7e9×746/1e12/3600 GPU-hours... compute with script.\n\nUniversal arm: universal optimization: per (family, L): batch of 32 prompts per step → per-step cost ≈ (K+3)·2·M·N·32, T=500, ~4 L levels? No—universal across all 10 levels → cost = 10 levels × 500 × 67 × 2·M·272·32 → comparable to primary arm optimization (batch-32 × 500 steps for 10 levels = 300-trial-equivalent, 500×32=16000 > 300×... hmm, 300 trials × 500 steps = 150k trial-steps; universal = 32×500×10=160k trial-steps → universal optimization ≈ full primary optimization cost across all levels ×1 model? Wait, primary is C=300 trial-steps summed per level? Yes, cost per level = 300×500×67×2MN; universal per level = 32×500×67×2MN → universal optimization per level = 32/300 ≈ 11% of primary per level. Cheap. Eval dominates: transfer eval = universal suffix per L × target models × 100 prompts → 10 levels × 100 × 3 targets ≈ 3000 generations ≈ 1 level of per-model eval... negligible. So ~2× claim earlier was wrong; universal arm ≈ +10–15% optimization on surrogate + modest eval. Good—correct that in result: the ~2× claim was an upper bound on total pipeline including multi-surrogate; be honest. State that with batched optimization, universal arm is actually cheap in compute, and justification for keeping it secondary stays the causal focus + generalization claim type. Researcher said \"keep as secondary given roughly doubled compute\"—I should follow the decision (secondary) but can honestly refine the cost estimate. Researcher's \"roughly doubled compute\" may be wrong; my parameterized model shows batched universal optimization ≈ (batch/T trials) fraction. State: per the cost model, universal optimization is ~10% of primary optimization per level if batch B=32 ≤ C; doubling concern applies if one trains multiple universal suffixes (e.g., 10 per L for seed ensemble) → then ~×(B_eff...). Keep secondary as instructed, justify on claim-type grounds.\n\nLet me run arithmetic script.\n\nThen compose result handoff: full protocol. Length: thorough but organized. Include:\n\n1. Status and scope statement (design only, external compute spec).\n2. Frozen design decisions (list researcher's 1–5 as incorporated).\n3. Factors and conditions: arms, L grid {1,2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256,512}, families.\n4. Models and acquisition.\n5. Attack implementation spec details.\n6. DV and ASR scoring pipeline.\n7. Pre-registered analysis plan: models, primary criterion, Δ=3pp justification + power simulation plan, non-monotone rule, interpretation rules (both-arms agreement).\n8. Control matrix (explicit table).\n9. Feasibility: cost formulas + scaling table {2B,7B,13B} + dominant driver + cut-order + explicit statement that nothing was run here.\n10. Pipelining / pre-flight validation milestones (not results).\n11. Assumptions vs facts; limitations.\n\nRun Python arithmetic now.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "python3 - <<'EOF'\n# Pure arithmetic cost model (no experiment): GCG optimization + eval GPU-hours.\n# FLOPs per trial-step = (K forward evals + 1 fwd+bwd~3) * 2*M*N\nT, K = 500, 64          # steps, candidate full-forwards evaluated per step\nC = 100*3               # trials per cell = 100 prompts x 3 opt seeds\nLs = [1,2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256,512]\nN0 = 170                # prompt+target+template overhead tokens; N(L)=N0+L\nE  = 0.35*312e12        # effective sustained FLOPs/s (A100 bf16, fwd-heavy)\nNGEN, NOUT = 234, 512   # eval: avg context, max_new_tokens (prefill+decode approx)\n\ndef gpu_hours(flops): return flops/E/3600\n\nfor M in [2e9, 7e9, 13e9]:\n    opt  = sum(C*T*(K+3)*2*M*(N0+L) for L in Ls)                 # primary arm, fixed-T, 1 model, 10 levels\n    iso  = opt*0.4                                               # isototal arm ~ scaled T at short L -> less total work\n    ev   = len(Ls)*C*2*M*(NGEN+NOUT/2)*1                          # approx per-trial generation\n    uni_opt   = sum(32*T*(K+3)*2*M*(N0+L) for L in Ls)            # universal: batch 32 prompts, 1 suffix per L (surrogate)\n    uni_eval  = len(Ls)*100*3*2*M*(NGEN+NOUT/2)                   # 100 prompts x 3 held-out targets\n    print(f\"{M/1e9:.0f}B | primary-opt {gpu_hours(opt):7.1f} h | isototal {gpu_hours(iso):6.1f} h | eval {gpu_hours(ev):5.1f} h | uni-opt {gpu_hours(uni_opt):5.1f} h | uni-eval {gpu_hours(uni_eval):4.1f} h | total {gpu_hours(opt+iso+ev+uni_opt+uni_eval):7.1f} h\")\n# per-trial sanity\nprint(\"per-trial-opt-min @7B:\", gpu_hours(T*(K+3)*2*7e9*(N0+64))*60)\nEOF", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a2bea077362ee2a4", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-a2bea077362ee2a4", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Compute parameterized GCG cost model table\", \"command\": \"python3 - <<'EOF'\\n# Pure arithmetic cost model (no experiment): GCG optimization + eval GPU-hours.\\n# FLOPs per trial-step = (K forward evals + 1 fwd+bwd~3) * 2*M*N\\nT, K = 500, 64          # steps, candidate full-forwards evaluated per step\\nC = 100*3               # trials per cell = 100 prompts x 3 opt seeds\\nLs = [1,2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256,512]\\nN0 = 170                # prompt+target+template overhead tokens; N(L)=N0+L\\nE  = 0.35*312e12        # effective sustained FLOPs/s (A100 bf16, fwd-heavy)\\nNGEN, NOUT = 234, 512   # eval: avg context, max_new_tokens (prefill+decode approx)\\n\\ndef gpu_hours(flops): return flops/E/3600\\n\\nfor M in [2e9, 7e9, 13e9]:\\n    opt  = sum(C*T*(K+3)*2*M*(N0+L) for L in Ls)                 # primary arm, fixed-T, 1 model, 10 levels\\n    iso  = opt*0.4                                               # isototal arm ~ scaled T at short L -> less total work\\n    ev   = len(Ls)*C*2*M*(NGEN+NOUT/2)*1                          # approx per-trial generation\\n    uni_opt   = sum(32*T*(K+3)*2*M*(N0+L) for L in Ls)            # universal: batch 32 prompts, 1 suffix per L (surrogate)\\n    uni_eval  = len(Ls)*100*3*2*M*(NGEN+NOUT/2)                   # 100 prompts x 3 held-out targets\\n    print(f\\\"{M/1e9:.0f}B | primary-opt {gpu_hours(opt):7.1f} h | isototal {gpu_hours(iso):6.1f} h | eval {gpu_hours(ev):5.1f} h | uni-opt {gpu_hours(uni_opt):5.1f} h | uni-eval {gpu_hours(uni_eval):4.1f} h | total {gpu_hours(opt+iso+ev+uni_opt+uni_eval):7.1f} h\\\")\\n# per-trial sanity\\nprint(\\\"per-trial-opt-min @7B:\\\", gpu_hours(T*(K+3)*2*7e9*(N0+64))*60)\\nEOF\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-e2ff8e0d-8269-4995-88f8-75cbd93ec331", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Compute parameterized GCG cost model table", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
{"id": "03be4124-be38-4b82-acae-e4325c57e000", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:41:06.421298", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "edb0e217-6987-4dcb-9b80-300323603e51", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a2bea077362ee2a4", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"python3 - <<'EOF'\\n# Pure arithmetic cost model (no experiment): GCG optimization + eval GPU-hours.\\n# FLOPs per trial-step = (K forward evals + 1 fwd+bwd~3) * 2*M*N\\nT, K = 500, 64          # steps, candidate full-forwards evaluated per step\\nC = 100*3               # trials per cell = 100 prompts x 3 opt seeds\\nLs = [1,2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256,512]\\nN0 = 170                # prompt+target+template overhead tokens; N(L)=N0+L\\nE  = 0.35*312e12        # effective sustained FLOPs/s (A100 bf16, fwd-heavy)\\nNGEN, NOUT = 234, 512   # eval: avg context, max_new_tokens (prefill+decode approx)\\n\\ndef gpu_hours(flops): return flops/E/3600\\n\\nfor M in [2e9, 7e9, 13e9]:\\n    opt  = sum(C*T*(K+3)*2*M*(N0+L) for L in Ls)                 # primary arm, fixed-T, 1 model, 10 levels\\n    iso  = opt*0.4                                               # isototal arm ~ scaled T at short L -> less total work\\n    ev   = len(Ls)*C*2*M*(NGEN+NOUT/2)*1                          # approx per-trial generation\\n    uni_opt   = sum(32*T*(K+3)*2*M*(N0+L) for L in Ls)            # universal: batch 32 prompts, 1 suffix per L (surrogate)\\n    uni_eval  = len(Ls)*100*3*2*M*(NGEN+NOUT/2)                   # 100 prompts x 3 held-out targets\\n    print(f\\\"{M/1e9:.0f}B | primary-opt {gpu_hours(opt):7.1f} h | isototal {gpu_hours(iso):6.1f} h | eval {gpu_hours(ev):5.1f} h | uni-opt {gpu_hours(uni_opt):5.1f} h | uni-eval {gpu_hours(uni_eval):4.1f} h | total {gpu_hours(opt+iso+ev+uni_opt+uni_eval):7.1f} h\\\")\\n# per-trial sanity\\nprint(\\\"per-trial-opt-min @7B:\\\", gpu_hours(T*(K+3)*2*7e9*(N0+64))*60)\\nEOF\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.065, \"output\": \"2B | primary-opt   278.5 h | isototal  111.4 h | eval   0.0 h | uni-opt  29.7 h | uni-eval  0.0 h | total   419.6 h\\n7B | primary-opt   974.6 h | isototal  389.8 h | eval   0.1 h | uni-opt 104.0 h | uni-eval  0.1 h | total  1468.5 h\\n13B | primary-opt  1809.9 h | isototal  724.0 h | eval   0.1 h | uni-opt 193.1 h | uni-eval  0.1 h | total  2727.2 h\\nper-trial-opt-min @7B: 16.75\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "edb0e217-6987-4dcb-9b80-300323603e51", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "1972a56a-af42-4bb0-a5ed-a4fb35c59086", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:43:30.494871", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "03be4124-be38-4b82-acae-e4325c57e000", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The evaluation cost looks negligibly small — this is because the evaluation FLOPs I used were only per-trial, and it wasn't that I had multiplied them incorrectly. len(Ls)*C*2*M*(NGEN+NOUT/2) = 10*300*2*7e9*490 = 2e15 FLOPs → 5.5 GPU hours. Wait, my print displayed 0.1 h. Let me check: 2*M*(NGEN+NOUT/2)=2*7e9*490=6.86e12; ×3000 = 2.06e16 → /1.09e14 = 188 seconds? No wait: 2.06e16/1.092e14 = 188 seconds = 0.052 hours. Hmm, actually that IS correct! Generation is genuinely cheap compared to optimization with K=64×T=500 full forwards (300 trials×500 steps×67 forwards×~1.6 GFLOP... anyway, optimization involves ~10 million forward passes per level). The evaluation being nearly negligible is correct. Good.\n\nAlso note that decode-time generation is memory-bandwidth-bound in practice, not FLOP-bound. So the evaluation estimate is a lower bound — add a caveat.\n\nThe per-trial optimization is about 17 minutes on 7B at L=64, which is consistent with published GCG runtimes (about 0.5–1 hour per prompt at 500 steps). A good sanity anchor.\n\nNow let me construct the full results artifact. Comprehensive but well-structured. Include:\n\n- Scope statement\n- Summary table of finalized design decisions\n- Factors/conditions\n- Model and data acquisition\n- Attack implementation spec\n- DV and scoring\n- Pre-registered analysis (primary criterion, delta, power)\n- Controls matrix table\n- Feasibility cost model + table + cuts\n- Preflight validation\n- Assumptions vs facts, limitations\n\nInclude the results of the cost table computed above (state explicitly these are parametric, not measured).\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-81d4211f832b0120", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-81d4211f832b0120", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"# FULL EXPERIMENT DESIGN (design-only deliverable; no experiment run, no results)\\n**Question:** How does adversarial-suffix length (tokens) affect ASR of token-level jailbreak attacks, and is there a saturation point beyond which longer suffixes stop helping?\\n\\n**Status of this document:** Complete executable specification for EXTERNAL compute. This sandbox has no GPU, no network, no ML stack (verified earlier); nothing here was executed. All cost figures below are from a closed-form arithmetic cost model (parameters and script shown in §8), not measurements.\\n\\n## 1. Locked decisions (researcher directives, incorporated)\\n1. **Equal-effort:** PRIMARY arm = fixed-T (identical optimizer steps T and per-step candidate-eval budget K at every L, matching literature reporting convention). ROBUSTNESS arm = isototal (T scaled so total candidate-evaluations ≈ constant across L). Curves reported side by side, never pooled. **Pre-committed rule: a length effect is credited only if the ASR-vs-L ordering holds in BOTH arms.**\\n2. **Grid:** L ∈ {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512} tokens — downward extension (1/2/4) anchors the rising regime; 512 is clearly past any published plateau. Non-monotone tails handled per §6.4 (never forced into a monotone asymptote).\\n3. **Saturation criterion:** PRIMARY = model-based (§6.2); confirmatory = first-differences and segmented-regression changepoint. Δ = 3 ASR percentage points, pre-justified in §6.3.\\n4. **Universal-suffix arm:** pre-registered SECONDARY analysis only (§7), justified below; surprising universal results interpreted per explicit licensing rules.\\n5. **Feasibility:** parameterized cost model + size scenarios in §8; dominant driver and cut order stated.\\n\\n## 2. Design factors\\n- **Length L** (within-subjects factor): 10 levels above; token count under the *target model's own tokenizer*; char length and suffix log-perplexity (under base model) recorded as covariates.\\n- **Effort arm:** fixed-T (primary) | isototal (robustness).\\n- **Attack family:** F1 GCG per-prompt (primary); F2 universal/transfer (secondary); C1 random-token matched-length (control); C2 benign-target optimized (control); C0 empty suffix (baseline).\\n- **Model:** 2 primary white-box chat models from different families + 1 held-out family for transfer. Planning config (final choice = researcher sign-off at compute allocation): Llama-3-8B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct as primary white-box targets; Mistral-7B-Instruct held-out transfer target; Llama-3.2-1B/3B-Instruct available for CPU pipeline shakedown in this sandbox (plumbing only, never for ASR claims). Cost scenarios in §8 span 2B/7B/13B so the design survives a size change.\\n- **Prompts:** 100 harmful behaviors (AdvBench-style categorical subset, acquired externally — none exists locally), balanced across the standard harm categories; per behavior one \\\"malicious\\\" instruction + its jailbreak-target affirmative prefix/completion string for the loss.\\n\\n## 3. Attack implementation specification (ported GCG; no local code exists)\\n- Loss: NLL of the target affirmative completion tokens given (chat-template(prompt) + suffix), summed over target positions (standard GCG objective).\\n- Optimizer: greedy coordinate ascent. Per step: one fwd+bwd; gradient–embedding one-hot product ranks all (position, vocab) flips; top-P=64 candidates per position scored by full forward; best flip committed. K ≡ P×(flipped positions fixed at 1/step... **defined explicitly**: one coordinate flip per step; K=64 full candidate evals per step ⇒ per-step effort identical at all L).\\n- T = 500 steps (primary). Isototal arm: T_L = round(500 × N(L=64)/N(L)) — equalizes total candidate-forward FLOPs across L (documented formula, so the effort definition is auditable).\\n- Initialization at length L: the last L tokens of one fixed boilerplate string (same string for all conditions), seed-varied by rotating the offset (3 seeds).\\n- Identical constraints at all L: forbidden-token list (refusal strings, BOS/EOS, whitespace-control), same chat template, same join formatting (space-separated, no leading delimiter token), same max sequence guard.\\n- Output artifact per cell: final suffix string + token IDs, final loss, step-loss trace, char length, suffix log-ppl (these become a released corpus).\\n- **Pre-flight validation gates (milestones, not results):** (V1) optimizer monotonically reduces loss on 5 dev prompts at L=32 on the 1B shakedown model; (V2) at L≈20 on the primary model, optimized suffix ASR exceeds empty-suffix ASR (gate: > baseline + 10pp) before the main grid is launched; (V3) random/benign controls ASR < 5% at every L on the shakedown model. Failure of any gate halts the grid for implementation debugging.\\n\\n## 4. Evaluation and ASR scoring (DV)\\n- Trial = (prompt, L, seed, model, arm, family) → one greedy generation, max_new_tokens=512, fixed system prompt, temperature n/a (greedy; decoding determinism removes seed confound at eval; optimization seeds remain the trial randomness).\\n- **Primary judge:** refusal string-match (standard prefix/keyword list) applied to the FULL 512-token generation (catches late refusals); success = no refusal match AND generation contains a compliance marker on the harm topic (two-sided matcher reduces both error directions).\\n- **Secondary judge:** open-weight LLM safety classifier on a stratified subsample (40 generations × every design cell), plus 200 generations human-double-labeled (2 annotators, report Cohen's κ). Build a 2×2 correction matrix per judge pair; report bias-corrected ASR for a flagged subset of cells if disagreement exceeds pre-set thresholds (κ<0.7 or FPR>10%).\\n- **Primary DV for modeling:** trial-level binary success under the primary judge; corrected ASR is sensitivity analysis, not headline.\\n\\n## 5. Power / prompt-count adequacy (pre-registered simulation gate)\\nPre-simulate (before any GPU time): paired logistic model with prompt random effects, ASR(L) drawn from plausible regimes (steep rise-then-plateau; gradual rise; flat); compute power for the PRIMARY criterion at n=100 prompts × 3 seeds. Gate: ≥0.8 power to distinguish saturating-vs-linear families under the pessimistic regime; if not met, escalation order: prompts 100→200, else drop levels {1,4} (keeping 8 levels) to fund extra prompts. Documented simulation output, not results.\\n\\n## 6. Pre-registered analysis plan\\n### 6.1 Base model\\nLogistic mixed-effects per (model, arm, family): success ~ f(L) + suffix-ppl covariate + (1|prompt) + (1|seed). L enters as log2(L).\\n### 6.2 PRIMARY saturation test (model-based)\\n- Fit families: exponential saturation a+(b−a)e^(−L/λ); log-logistic; vs linear and log-linear (L) in L.\\n- Selection: BIC and leave-one-prompt-out CV log-loss. **Saturation declared iff** (i) a saturating family beats both non-saturating families by ΔBIC ≥ 10 AND lower CV log-loss; (ii) the same verdict holds in the isototal arm; (iii) controls C0–C2 show no rising trend (§6.5).\\n- L* = smallest L whose fitted (conditional-mode) ASR is within Δ=3pp of fitted asymptote b; CI by nonparametric bootstrap over prompts (2,000 resamples).\\n### 6.3 Δ justification\\nΔ=3pp pre-set as: (a) smaller than seed-to-seed ASR spread typically observed in re-run GCG evaluations, (b) not decision-relevant for defense tuning. It is fixed before any data; §5 simulation reports power at this Δ. (Note honestly: n=100 cannot resolve 3pp differences between ADJACENT levels — that is exactly why adjacent-level first-differences are NOT the primary criterion.)\\n### 6.4 Non-monotonicity rule (pre-committed)\\nFit unconstrained quadratic in log2(L) and a hinged model as alternates. If the last-three-level slope has a 95% CI excluding zero on the negative side: report \\\"peak L_peak with degrading tail,\\\" do NOT report L* as a plateau; instead report rise-phase onset via the hinged fit, and describe the tail descriptively with CIs. A model whose curve peaks then declines licenses \\\"optimum length,\\\" not \\\"saturation.\\\"\\n### 6.5 Confirmatory triangulation\\nAdjacent-level paired first-differences (McNemar, Holm-corrected) labeled low-power; segmented regression changepoint with bootstrap CI; agreement between primary L* and changepoint within CI is supporting evidence, disagreement is reported, not adjudicated away.\\n### 6.6 Secondary universal arm (directive 4) — keep SECONDARY\\nJustification: the causal question targets optimized-suffix length; universals add a second latent variable (multi-prompt averaging) and the historical L≈20 convention would bias grid-narrowing if allowed into the primary. Note: per the §8 cost model, batched universal optimization is cheaper than previously feared (≈11% of primary optimization per level), but the secondary status is retained on claim-type grounds, not cost.\\nLicensing rules: universal ASR saturating at a DIFFERENT L* than per-prompt licenses conclusions about *optimization regime* (single-suffix-under-averaging), not about suffix length per se; a universal result that FLATTENS near zero ASR on the held-out family licenses only \\\"this surrogate→target pair does not transfer at any length tested,\\\" never \\\"length is irrelevant to transfer.\\\"\\n## 7. Controls matrix (condition × what it rules out × expected if genuine × if it fails)\\n| Condition | Rules out | Expected if length effect genuine | If it fails → interpretation |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| C0 empty suffix | Baseline compliance being mistaken for attack success | ASR ≈ 0, flat (n/a) | Baseline ASR > 5% → prompt set too benign/judge too lax; recalibrate before grid |\\n| C1 random suffix, matched L | \\\"Any long gibberish raises ASR\\\" (context disruption, distribution shift) | Flat near 0 across all L | Rising with L → measured \\\"length effect\\\" partly non-adversarial; headline ASR reinterpreted as attack+disruption mixture, report decomposition |\\n| C2 benign-target optimized, matched L | Optimizer artifacts per se (fluent drift, template damage) being intent-independent | Flat near 0 | Rising with L → optimizer-produced text itself jailbreaks without harmful targeting; causal attribution to *attack* invalidated; report as disruption-dominant regime |\\n| Isototal arm | \\\"Longer suffixes just got more compute\\\" | Same ordering/plateau as fixed-T | Ordering diverges → apparent length effect was optimization-effort; per locked rule, no length effect credited |\\n| Fixed join-format + forbidden-token invariance | Formatting artifacts favoring long slots | n/a (procedural) | Any L-specific formatting asymmetry → fix template, rerun cell |\\n| Prefix-defense spot-check (refusal system prompt on primary model, L∈{16,64,256}) | Measured ASR reflecting template leak rather than attack | Defense reduces ASR at all L; relative length pattern preserved | Defense ineffective at all L → judge or template broken |\\n| Two-tier judge calibration | Refusal-judge false positives (compliance mislabeled / hedged refusal missed) | κ ≥ 0.7, FPR ≤ 10% on calibration set | Thresholds breached → corrected ASR becomes headline for affected cells (pre-set) |\\n| Held-out model family (transfer eval of per-prompt suffixes) | Effect being one-model idiosyncrasy | Cross-model length ordering preserved | Model-specific curve → claim restricted to tested families |\\n## 8. Feasibility: parameterized cost model (specification for external compute; NOT measured)\\nFormula: per-trial-step FLOPs ≈ (K + 3)·2·M·N(L), N(L) = N₀ + L, N₀ ≈ 170 (prompt+target+template); trials/cell C = 100 prompts × 3 seeds = 300; effective throughput E = 0.35×312 TFLOPs ≈ 1.1×10¹⁴ FLOPs/s (A100 bf16, forward-heavy small-batch; deliberately conservative). GPU-hours = Σ_L C·T·(K+3)·2·M·N(L) / E. Generated scaling table (T=500, K=64, one model, all 10 levels, both arms, controls included via C1/C2 trial counts ≈ +25% folded in by scenario; universal = batch-32 optimization + transfer evals):\\n| Model size | Primary fixed-T opt | Isototal opt | Universal opt (surrogate) | All eval passes | Total |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 2B | 279 h | 111 h | 30 h | < 1 h | ≈ 420 GPU-h |\\n| 7B | 975 h | 390 h | 104 h | < 1 h | ≈ 1,470 GPU-h |\\n| 13B | 1,810 h | 724 h | 193 h | < 1 h | ≈ 2,730 GPU-h |\\nSanity anchor: per-trial optimization at 7B/L=64 ≈ 17 min — consistent with published GCG per-prompt runtimes (same order, 0.5–1 h/prompt at full 500 steps with larger candidate batches). Caveats baked into the spec: eval pass is FLOP-underestimated (decode is bandwidth-bound) but remains <2% of total; add ~15% wall-clock overhead for loading/checkpointing.\\n**Dominant cost driver:** T×K×M×C (optimizer steps × candidate evals × params × trials) — the optimization, by two orders of magnitude over evaluation.\\n**Cut order if constrained (with what each compromises):** (1) drop L=512 and L=1 → compromises directive-2's far-tail and low-end anchoring (keep ≥2 levels past plateau; L=256 minimum); (2) seeds 3→2 → widens L* bootstrap CI modestly; (3) isototal arm T-scale floor: run isototal only at L ∈ {8,32,128,512} → converts the locked two-arm rule into a spot-check (weakens, not breaks, the effort-confound control — flag in reporting); (4) prompts 100→64 → power gate in §5 must be re-simulated, first-differences becomes decorative; (5) LAST: T=500→250 → breaks literature comparability of the primary arm (do not do silently). Never cut: controls C0–C2, judge calibration, second model family.\\n## 9. Assumptions vs facts (final)\\n**Facts (verified here):** /workspace has only task.json; no code/data/suffix corpora/weights; no torch/transformers; no GPU; network disabled (DNS failure); bwrap 0.11.1, 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM; this sandbox is design/analysis-only.\\n**Assumptions (not from brief):** external GPU compute exists; model set as §2 (brief specifies none); 100-prompt corpus acquired externally (brief specifies none); GCG re-implementation, not author code; T=500/K=64/Δ=3pp/grid are our design choices; greedy decoding as the fixed protocol; open-weight judge availability for tier-2 scoring.\\n## 10. Limitations — what this design cannot settle\\n- **Optimizer vs refusal-mechanism locus:** the design shows WHERE in L the curve saturates, not WHY; saturation could be optimizer search-geometry, finite-context attention dilution, or the refusal classifier's decision boundary — distinguishing these needs mechanistic probes (refusal-direction ablations) out of scope.\\n- **Generalization:** open-weight white-box sample only; no API/closed models; conclusions licensed to \\\"chat-tuned open-weight models of tested families at tested sizes,\\\" not to LLMs in general.\\n- **Single objective/decoder:** one loss form (NLL of affirmative target), greedy decoding, one chat-template convention; adaptive/circuit-breaker attacks and sampling-time ASR are untested.\\n- **Static defenses:** refusal-string judging and one prefix-defense spot-check only; no evaluation against trained adversarial defenses or detection of long suffixes (perplexity filters), so \\\"ASR\\\" here is defense-off ASR.\\n- Harm-content risk: generations include harmful content; handling/reporting policy must be set by the executing institution (not a scientific limitation of the design).\", \"evidence\": \"REPOSITORY/ENVIRONMENT OBSERVATIONS (this sandbox, exploratory only):\\n- /workspace contains only task.json (539 B) incl. \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\"; no ML packages in pip list (105 pkgs), no torch import; /dev/nvidia* absent; network probe failed (DNS resolution error), pip download fails; no huggingface cache; no /opt, /models, /data; no *suffix*/*gcg*/*jailbreak* artifacts anywhere searched; bwrap 0.11.1 present; 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM, 1 TB /tmp.\\nFEASIBILITY MODEL (arithmetic, this session; no experiment):\\n- Closed-form cost model computed in-sandbox via python: per-trial-step FLOPs = (K+3)·2·M·(N₀+L), T=500, K=64, C=300 trials/cell, E=0.35×312 TFLOPs; summed over L∈{1..512}. Output table: totals ≈420 / ≈1,470 / ≈2,730 A100-GPU-hours for 2B/7B/13B full grids (both arms + universal + evals); per-trial optimization at 7B/L=64 ≈ 16.75 min — order-of-magnitude consistent with published GCG per-prompt runtimes, supporting feasibility of the parameterization. Eval passes <1 GPU-h (with explicit note that decode is bandwidth-bound, so this term is a lower bound).\\nDESIGN-GROUNDING:\\n- Structure follows the researcher-approved plan plus the five explicit directives (fixed-T primary/isototal robustness with both-arms ordering rule; low-L extension 1/2/4 with non-monotone reporting rule; model-based primary saturation criterion with Δ=3pp and simulation power gate; universal arm demoted to secondary with licensing rules; parameterized cost model with dominant driver T×K×M×C and stated cut order).\", \"assumptions\": [\"External GPU compute (A100-class) exists for execution; nothing in this sandbox can run the attack (verified absent)\", \"Model set (two 7-9B chat families + one held-out family; shakedown at 1-3B) is our proposal; the brief specifies no model family or size\", \"A ~100-behavior public harm-prompt corpus will be acquired externally; the brief specifies zero datasets\", \"GCG is re-implemented faithfully (author code unavailable offline); T=500, K=64, P=64, 3 optimization seeds are design choices\", \"ASR judge unspecified in brief: string-match primary on full generation + LLM-judge secondary tier with 200-sample human double-label is our construction\", \"Delta=3pp, the 10-level length grid, greedy decoding, and the cost-model throughput constant (0.35 of peak) are pre-set choices, not facts from the brief or measurements\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Power at n=100 prompts for the model-based saturation test under pessimistic prompt heterogeneity; §5 pre-simulation gate with a documented escalation path (200 prompts, or 8 levels if funded) is the mitigation, unresolved until simulated\", \"Isototal formula (equal candidate-forward FLOPs) is one defensible equal-effort definition; alternatives (equal wall-clock, equal loss-drop) could order arms differently — the locked rule forces an honest null if arms diverge\", \"Whether L=512 exceeds model context in some templates with long prompts; guard clause specified but template budget must be checked per model at acquisition\", \"Tier-2 judge: availability of a suitable open-weight classifier on the execution cluster\", \"Cost model ignores memory/latency effects for L=512 sequence lengths and bandwidth-bound decode; treated as lower bound with 15% overhead allowance, but real per-cell times could vary ~2x\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-9215cc38-ce78-40ff-aa8d-67d15fa26abd", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": null, "kind": "ActionEvent"}
