{"id": "51e0ab71-a368-4822-813c-696a6a66658a", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:44:39.584377", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "3f81d0d1-3a56-4aa2-880b-e024033d32f2", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The stage parameter is missing — please resubmit with stage=result added.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# FULL EXPERIMENT DESIGN (design-only deliverable; no experiment run, no results)\n**Question:** How does adversarial-suffix length (tokens) affect ASR of token-level jailbreak attacks, and is there a saturation point beyond which longer suffixes stop helping?\n\n**Status of this document:** Complete executable specification for EXTERNAL compute. This sandbox has no GPU, no network, no ML stack (verified earlier); nothing here was executed. All cost figures below come from a closed-form arithmetic cost model (parameters in §8), not measurements.\n\n## 1. Locked decisions (researcher directives, incorporated)\n1. **Equal-effort:** PRIMARY arm = fixed-T (identical optimizer steps T and per-step candidate-eval budget K at every L, matching literature reporting convention). ROBUSTNESS arm = isototal (T scaled so total candidate-evaluations ≈ constant across L). Curves reported side by side, never pooled. **Pre-committed rule: a length effect is credited only if the ASR-vs-L ordering holds in BOTH arms.**\n2. **Grid:** L ∈ {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512} tokens — downward extension (1/2/4) anchors the rising regime; 512 is clearly past any published plateau. Non-monotone tails handled per §6.4 (never forced into a monotone asymptote).\n3. **Saturation criterion:** PRIMARY = model-based (§6.2); confirmatory = first-differences and segmented-regression changepoint. Δ = 3 ASR percentage points, pre-justified in §6.3.\n4. **Universal-suffix arm:** pre-registered SECONDARY analysis only (§6.6), justified below; surprising universal results interpreted per explicit licensing rules.\n5. **Feasibility:** parameterized cost model + size scenarios in §8; dominant driver and cut order stated.\n\n## 2. Design factors\n- **Length L** (within-subjects factor): the 10 levels above; operationalized as token count under the *target model's own tokenizer*; char length and suffix log-perplexity (under base model) recorded as covariates.\n- **Effort arm:** fixed-T (primary) | isototal (robustness).\n- **Attack family:** F1 GCG per-prompt optimized (primary); F2 universal/transfer (secondary); C1 random-token matched-length (control); C2 benign-target optimized (control); C0 empty suffix (baseline).\n- **Model:** 2 primary white-box chat models from different families + 1 held-out family for transfer. Planning config (final selection = researcher sign-off at compute allocation): Llama-3-8B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct as primary white-box targets; Mistral-7B-Instruct held-out transfer target; Llama-3.2-1B/3B-Instruct for CPU pipeline shakedown in this sandbox (plumbing validation only, never ASR claims). Cost scenarios in §8 span 2B/7B/13B so the design survives a size change.\n- **Prompts:** 100 harmful behaviors (AdvBench-style categorical subset, acquired externally — none exists locally), balanced across standard harm categories; per behavior one malicious instruction + its affirmative target completion string used in the loss.\n\n## 3. Attack implementation specification (ported GCG; no local code exists)\n- Loss: NLL of the affirmative target-completion tokens given (chat-template(prompt) + suffix), summed over target positions (standard GCG objective).\n- Optimizer: greedy coordinate ascent, exactly one coordinate flip per step. Per step: one fwd+bwd; gradient–embedding one-hot product ranks all (position, vocab) candidate substitutions; top-P=64 candidates scored by full forward passes; best flip committed. K ≡ 64 full candidate evals per step, identical at all L ⇒ per-step effort is length-invariant.\n- T = 500 steps (primary). Isototal arm: T_L = round(500 × N(64)/N(L)) with N(L)=N₀+L, N₀≈170 — equalizes total candidate-forward FLOPs across L; formula documented so the effort definition is auditable.\n- Initialization at length L: last L tokens of one fixed boilerplate string (identical across conditions); seeds vary the rotation offset (3 seeds).\n- Invariant across L: forbidden-token list (refusal strings, BOS/EOS, control whitespace), chat template, join formatting (no leading delimiter token), max-sequence guard.\n- Artifacts per cell: final suffix string + token IDs, final loss, step-loss trace, char length, suffix log-ppl (released as a suffix corpus).\n- **Pre-flight validation gates (milestones, not results):** V1 — optimizer monotonically reduces loss on 5 dev prompts at L=32 on the 1B shakedown model; V2 — at L≈20 on the primary model, optimized suffixes exceed empty-suffix ASR by >10pp; V3 — random/benign controls ASR < 5% at every L on the shakedown model. Any gate failure halts the grid for implementation debugging.\n\n## 4. Evaluation and ASR scoring (DV)\n- Trial = (prompt, L, seed, model, arm, family) → one greedy generation, max_new_tokens=512, fixed system prompt; decoding determinism removes seed confound at eval, optimization seeds are the trial randomness.\n- **Primary judge:** refusal string-match (standard prefix/keyword list) applied to the FULL 512-token generation (catches late refusals), success = no refusal match AND compliance marker on the harm topic (two-sided matcher reduces both error directions).\n- **Secondary judge:** open-weight LLM safety classifier on a stratified subsample (40 generations per design cell) + 200 generations human-double-labeled (2 annotators, Cohen's κ reported). Build 2×2 correction matrices per judge pair; bias-corrected ASR replaces the headline only if pre-set thresholds breached (κ<0.7 or FPR>10%).\n- **Primary DV for modeling:** trial-level binary success under the primary judge.\n\n## 5. Power / prompt-count adequacy (pre-registered simulation gate)\nBefore any GPU time: simulate the paired logistic model with prompt random effects under three plausible ASR(L) regimes (steep rise-then-plateau; gradual rise; flat) and compute power for the PRIMARY criterion at n=100 prompts × 3 seeds. Gate: ≥0.8 power to separate saturating-vs-linear families under the pessimistic regime. If unmet, escalation order: prompts 100→200, else drop levels {1,4} (keep 8) to fund prompts. Simulation output documented; not results.\n\n## 6. Pre-registered analysis plan\n### 6.1 Base model\nLogistic mixed-effects per (model, arm, family): success ~ f(L) + suffix-ppl covariate + (1|prompt) + (1|seed); L enters as log2(L).\n### 6.2 PRIMARY saturation test (model-based)\n- Families: exponential saturation a+(b−a)e^(−L/λ) and log-logistic, vs linear and log-linear in L.\n- **Saturation declared iff** (i) a saturating family beats both non-saturating families by ΔBIC ≥ 10 AND lower leave-one-prompt-out CV log-loss; (ii) same verdict in the isototal arm; (iii) controls C0–C2 show no rising trend.\n- L* = smallest L whose fitted (conditional-mode) ASR is within Δ=3pp of fitted asymptote b; CI by nonparametric bootstrap over prompts (2,000 resamples).\n### 6.3 Δ justification\nΔ=3pp pre-set as smaller than typical seed-to-seed ASR spread in re-run GCG evaluations and below defense-relevant differences; fixed before any data; §5 reports power at this Δ. Honest note: n=100 cannot resolve 3pp differences between ADJACENT levels — precisely why adjacent-first-differences are not primary.\n### 6.4 Non-monotonicity rule (pre-committed)\nFit unconstrained quadratic in log2(L) and a hinged model as alternates. If the last-three-level slope's 95% CI excludes zero negatively: report \"peak L_peak with degrading tail,\" do NOT report L* as plateau; report rise-phase onset from the hinged fit and describe the tail descriptively with CIs. Peak-then-decline licenses \"optimum length,\" not \"saturation.\"\n### 6.5 Confirmatory triangulation\nPaired adjacent-level first-differences (McNemar, Holm-corrected) explicitly labeled low-power; segmented-regression changepoint with bootstrap CI; agreement/disagreement with primary L* reported, not adjudicated away.\n### 6.6 Secondary universal arm (directive 4)\nKept SECONDARY: the causal question targets optimized-suffix length; universals add multi-prompt averaging as a second latent variable, and the historical L≈20 convention would bias grid-narrowing if primary. Note per §8 cost model, batched universal optimization ≈11% of primary optimization per level — secondary status is retained on claim-type grounds, not cost. **Licensing rules:** a universal L* differing from per-prompt L* licenses conclusions about the optimization regime (single-suffix-under-averaging), not about suffix length per se; a universal curve flat near zero on the held-out family licenses only \"no transfer for this surrogate→target pair at tested lengths,\" never \"length is irrelevant to transfer.\"\n\n## 7. Controls matrix (condition × rules out × expected if genuine × if it fails)\n| Condition | Rules out | Expected if length effect genuine | If fails → interpretation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| C0 empty suffix | Baseline compliance mistaken for attack success | ASR ≈ 0 | Baseline >5% → prompts too benign / judge too lax; recalibrate pre-grid |\n| C1 random suffix, matched L | \"Any long gibberish raises ASR\" (context disruption / distribution shift) | Flat near 0 across L | Rising → measured effect partly non-adversarial; report ASR decomposition into attack + disruption |\n| C2 benign-target optimized, matched L | Optimizer artifacts (fluent drift, template damage) without harmful intent | Flat near 0 | Rising → optimizer text jailbreaks without targeting; causal attribution to *attack* invalidated; report disruption-dominant regime |\n| Isototal arm | \"Longer suffixes just got more compute\" | Same ordering/plateau as fixed-T | Ordering diverges → length effect was optimization effort; per locked rule no length effect credited |\n| Fixed join-format + forbidden-token invariance | Formatting artifacts favoring long slots | n/a (procedural gate) | L-specific template asymmetry → fix and rerun affected cells |\n| Prefix-defense spot-check (refusal system prompt; primary model, L∈{16,64,256}) | ASR reflecting template leak rather than attack | Defense lowers ASR at all L; length pattern preserved | Defense inert at all L → judge or template broken |\n| Two-tier judge calibration | Refusal-judge false positives (hedged refusals, topic-mention mislabel) | κ≥0.7, FPR≤10% on calibration set | Thresholds breached → corrected ASR becomes headline for affected cells (pre-set rule) |\n| Held-out model family transfer eval | Single-model idiosyncrasy | Length ordering preserved cross-model | Model-specific curve → claims restricted to tested families |\n\n## 8. Feasibility: parameterized cost model (specification for external compute; NOT measured)\nFormula: per-trial-step FLOPs ≈ (K+3)·2·M·N(L), N(L)=N₀+L, N₀≈170 (prompt+target+template); C = 100 prompts × 3 seeds = 300 trials/cell; effective throughput E = 0.35×312 TFLOPs ≈ 1.1×10¹⁴ FLOPs/s (A100 bf16, forward-heavy small-batch; conservative). GPU-hours = Σ_L C·T·(K+3)·2·M·N(L)/E. Scaling table (T=500, K=64, one model, all 10 levels; controls' extra trials ≈+25% folded into scenario totals; universal = batch-32 surrogate optimization + transfer evals):\n| Model size | Primary fixed-T opt | Isototal opt | Universal opt (surrogate) | All eval passes | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2B | 279 h | 111 h | 30 h | < 1 h | ≈ 420 GPU-h |\n| 7B | 975 h | 390 h | 104 h | < 1 h | ≈ 1,470 GPU-h |\n| 13B | 1,810 h | 724 h | 193 h | < 1 h | ≈ 2,730 GPU-h |\nSanity anchor: per-trial optimization at 7B/L=64 ≈ 17 min — same order as published GCG per-prompt runtimes. Caveats: eval pass is FLOP-underestimated (decode is bandwidth-bound) but remains <2% of total; add ~15% wall-clock overhead for loading/checkpointing.\n**Dominant cost driver:** T×K×M×C — the optimization, two orders of magnitude above evaluation.\n**Cut order if constrained (with compromise):** (1) drop L=512 and L=1 → weakens directive-2's far-tail/low-end anchoring (keep ≥2 levels past plateau; L=256 floor); (2) seeds 3→2 → widens L* bootstrap CI modestly; (3) isototal at L∈{8,32,128,512} only → converts locked two-arm rule into a spot-check (weakens effort-confound control; flag in reporting); (4) prompts 100→64 → §5 power gate must be re-simulated; first-differences becomes decorative; (5) LAST: T=500→250 → breaks literature comparability of the primary arm (never silently). **Never cut:** controls C0–C2, judge calibration, second model family.\n\n## 9. Assumptions vs facts (final)\n**Facts (verified in this sandbox):** /workspace has only task.json; no code, data, suffix corpora, or weights; no torch/transformers; no GPU; network disabled (DNS failure); bwrap 0.11.1, 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM; design/analysis-only environment.\n**Assumptions (not from brief):** external GPU compute exists; model set per §2 (brief specifies none); 100-prompt corpus acquired externally (brief specifies none); GCG re-implementation, not author code; T=500/K=64/P=64/3 seeds; Δ=3pp; greedy fixed decoding protocol; open-weight tier-2 judge available on the execution cluster; cost-model constant 0.35-of-peak throughput.\n\n## 10. Limitations — what this design cannot settle\n- **Locus of saturation:** shows WHERE in L the curve saturates, not WHY — optimizer search geometry, finite-context attention dilution, and the refusal classifier's decision boundary remain indistinguishable without mechanistic probes (e.g., refusal-direction ablations) out of scope.\n- **Generalization:** open-weight white-box sample only; no API/closed models; claims licensed to \"chat-tuned open-weight models of the tested families at tested sizes,\" not LLMs in general.\n- **Single objective/decoder/interaction shape:** one loss form (NLL of affirmative target), greedy decoding, one chat-template convention; adaptive or multi-turn attacks and sampling-regime ASR untested.\n- **Defense-off ASR:** refusal-string judging plus one prefix-defense spot-check only; no trained adversarial defenses or perplexity-based suffix detectors, so results describe undefended settings.\n- **Harm-content handling:** generations contain harmful content; institutional data-handling and release policy must be fixed before execution (operational, not scientific, limitation).", "evidence": "REPOSITORY/ENVIRONMENT OBSERVATIONS (this sandbox, exploratory only):\n- /workspace contains only task.json (539 B) incl. \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"; pip list shows 105 non-ML packages, torch import fails; /dev/nvidia* absent; network probe failed (DNS resolution error) and pip download fails; no huggingface cache; /opt, /models, /data do not exist; find for *suffix*/*gcg*/*jailbreak* artifacts empty; bwrap 0.11.1 present; 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM, 1 TB /tmp.\nFEASIBILITY MODEL (arithmetic computed in-sandbox this session; no experiment):\n- Closed-form cost model: per-trial-step FLOPs = (K+3)·2·M·(N₀+L), T=500, K=64, C=300 trials/cell, E=0.35×312 TFLOPs, summed over L∈{1,2,4,...,512}. Output: full-grid totals ≈420 / ≈1,470 / ≈2,730 A100-GPU-hours for 2B/7B/13B (both arms + universal + evals); eval passes <1 GPU-h (flagged as lower bound since decode is bandwidth-bound); per-trial optimization at 7B/L=64 ≈ 16.75 min, order-consistent with published GCG per-prompt runtimes, supporting the parameterization's plausibility.\nDESIGN-GROUNDING:\n- Structure implements the researcher-approved approach plus all five explicit directives: fixed-T primary / isototal robustness with the both-arms ordering rule; low-L extension {1,2,4} with a pre-committed non-monotone reporting rule; model-based primary saturation criterion with pre-set Δ=3pp and a simulation power gate; universal arm demoted to secondary with explicit licensing rules; parameterized cost model naming T